{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xk8kH3UXdvfisMo39xw8SC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CoRL OpenReview review form \"overall score\" rating scale 1-10 accept reject\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Default Review Form | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors\",\"url\":\"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors\"},{\"title\":\"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137\"},{\"title\":\"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3\"},{\"title\":\"Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2021 AN OPEN REVIEW OF OPENREVIEW:\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=Cn706AbJaKW\"},{\"title\":\"What Have We Learned from OpenReview?\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05885\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the OpenReview default review form rating scale. The rating scale uses a 1-10 scale with the following options: 10 (Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper), 9 (Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept), 8 (Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept), 7 (Good paper, accept), 6 (Marginally above acceptance threshold), 5 (Marginally below acceptance threshold), 4 (Ok but not good enough - rejection), 3 (Clear rejection), 2 (Strong rejection), and 1 (Trivial or wrong).\n\nRegarding CoRL specifically, the search results show that papers that receive all reviews in the \"reject\" range in the initial round will not proceed to the rebuttal phase, but the specific details of CoRL's exact rating scale weren't fully detailed in the available search results. The default OpenReview form provides a comprehensive 1-10 scale with clear accept/reject distinctions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"7a6d31c5-6583-49a9-b2ef-098686991a17","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:57:01.967Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CoRL OpenReview review form \"overall score\" rating scale 1-10 accept reject","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01CKgBYxN5Xj3RLYhEJDKZhD","content":[{"title":"Reviewer Guidelines — CoRL 2023","url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"},{"title":"Default Review Form | OpenReview","url":"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form"},{"title":"CoRL 2026 - Instruction for Authors","url":"https://www.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors"},{"title":"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137"},{"title":"OpenReviewer: A Specialized Large Language Model for Generating Critical Scientific Paper Reviews","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.11948v3"},{"title":"Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2021 AN OPEN REVIEW OF OPENREVIEW:","url":"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=Cn706AbJaKW"},{"title":"What Have We Learned from OpenReview?","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05885"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the OpenReview default review form rating scale. The rating scale uses a 1-10 scale with the following options: 10 (Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper), 9 (Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept), 8 (Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept), 7 (Good paper, accept), 6 (Marginally above acceptance threshold), 5 (Marginally below acceptance threshold), 4 (Ok but not good enough - rejection), 3 (Clear rejection), 2 (Strong rejection), and 1 (Trivial or wrong).\n\nRegarding CoRL specifically, the search results show that papers that receive all reviews in the \"reject\" range in the initial round will not proceed to the rebuttal phase, but the specific details of CoRL's exact rating scale weren't fully detailed in the available search results. The default OpenReview form provides a comprehensive 1-10 scale with clear accept/reject distinctions."],"durationSeconds":4.317902895}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0178TSmxA6BV3zEk4ixjatm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I now have the CoRL review rubric information. Let me consolidate what I know:\n\n**CoRL Review Dimensions** (from CoRL 2023 guidelines):\n1. Originality\n2. Quality\n3. Clarity\n4. Significance\n5. Relevance (to robot learning)\n6. Limitations\n\n**Overall Score Scale** (OpenReview standard, used by CoRL):\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n- 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n\n\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\n- 3: Clear rejection\n- 2: Strong rejection\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nWith a historical acceptance rate around 25-30%, I can now look at the actual paper. The title is \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3\" and it identifies CIVO (conditioning-induced value overestimation) as the core issue preventing temporal abstraction from working effectively in DreamerV3.\n\nThe paper demonstrates this through extensive experiments across four DMC environments with over 30 configurations, showing that their NVE metric reliably detects the problem with high accuracy. They test several solutions, finding that temporal convolutional networks can prevent CIVO on some environments but the approach isn't generalizable. Most of their diagnostic claims hold up empirically, though the TCN solution remains exploratory. Now I need to set up the review process using the standard template with two reviewers—one focused on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work—for a full review round.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":665,"cache_read_input_tokens":73451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":665},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"636c9592-edef-4176-9ec0-1fca04f0b2fb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0178TSmxA6BV3zEk4ixjatm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the full paper (15 pages) and review context. CoRL uses the standard OpenReview 1-10 scale with dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations. Now dispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":665,"cache_read_input_tokens":73451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":665},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"1a92ed12-1fdc-429b-a5ee-90e7180f93ef"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0178TSmxA6BV3zEk4ixjatm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AXHd5Jgakg897LgJx5Z2LG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Provide an independent, blind review.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n**Scoring scale** (OpenReview 1-10):\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal | 9: Top 15%, strong accept | 8: Top 50%, clear accept | 7: Good, accept | 6: Marginally above threshold | 5: Marginally below threshold | 4: Not good enough, reject | 3: Clear reject | 2: Strong reject | 1: Trivial/wrong\n\n**Review dimensions**: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance (to robot learning), Limitations\n\n**Acceptance rate**: ~25-30%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3\"\n\nThe paper investigates why variable-rate imagination (dt>1) consistently degrades performance in DreamerV3. Through 31+ configurations across 4 DMC environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop), the authors identify **conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO)** as the root failure mode: a trained temporal-step embedding (dt_embedding) enables the critic to exploit precise but inaccurate multi-step RSSM predictions, creating a positive feedback loop of value overestimation.\n\nKey contributions:\n1. **C1-C7 diagnostic framework**: Controlled factorial diagnosis isolating trained dt_embedding as necessary and sufficient condition for CIVO, validated across 4 environments with multi-seed replication.\n2. **NVE metric**: Normalized Value Excess at 1.5× threshold achieves AUROC=0.969 for detecting pathological overestimation.\n3. **Dual-pathway vulnerability (C6)**: trained dt_embedding creates collapse risk through both CIVO (Pathway A, value-driven) and non-CIVO policy instability (Pathway B, eval-driven). Restricting imagination to dt=1 does NOT prevent collapse (2/4 seeds collapse via Pathway B).\n4. **SGS failure (C7)**: Gradient-level selective gradient stopping fails 1/17 seeds across 3 variants and 2 environments. Compensatory amplification makes it worse in some cases.\n5. **TCN exploratory intervention (C8)**: Temporal Conditioning Noise σ=0.2 achieves first complete CIVO prevention on Walker Walk (NVE < 1.0× throughout, 90% baseline), but σ=0.1 insufficient, σ=0.5 destructive, Cheetah Run resists all dosages. Scoped as exploratory.\n\n## Methodology Details\n- Extends DreamerV3 with temporal-step embedding e_dt: Z+ → R^d conditioning the RSSM\n- Factorial design varies: (1) dt_embedding status (trained/untrained/frozen/removed), (2) temporal subsampling (ON/OFF), (3) imagination horizon (H=8 or H=15)\n- NVE(t) = (V_exp(t)/R_exp(t)) / (V_base(t)/R_base(t)), reliable at ≥35K steps where baseline v/e converged\n- Actor entropy tracked as secondary diagnostic\n\n## Key Tables from Paper\n- Table 1: Baselines across 4 environments (Walker 916, Cheetah 597, Cartpole 787, Hopper 230)\n- Table 2: CIVO severity cross-environment (Cartpole 1.8-5.2×, Walker 2.0-4.1×, Hopper 4-40×, Cheetah 5.8-108×)\n- Table 3: 2×2 dt_emb ablation (only trained+active → CIVO)\n- Table 5: Causal control: dt_embed_dim=0 prevents CIVO 0/10 across 3 environments\n- Table 7: NVE threshold classification: 1.5× achieves accuracy 0.94, sensitivity 0.94, specificity 0.94, F1 0.94\n- Table 11: RSSM fidelity gap — 008d reward gap 2.78× vs 008f 1.01×\n- Table 19: SGS 1/17 total, single \"success\" still has v/e ratio 0.056\n- Table 20: Claims summary (C1-C5 Supported, C6 Partially falsified, C7 Supported)\n- Table 21: TCN dose-response on Walker (σ=0.2 s0 NVE 0.908, eval 821 = 90% baseline)\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan includes ~20 imagined experiments covering:\n- Walker Walk: 008d (trained dt_emb, 2 seeds), 008f (no dt_emb, 4 seeds), 008a (untrained dt_emb, 3 seeds), 008e_v3 (dt=1 imag, 4 seeds), 015b (frozen dt_emb, 1 seed)\n- Cheetah Run: baseline (3 seeds), trained dt_emb (3 seeds), no dt_emb (3 seeds), untrained dt_emb (3 seeds)\n- Cartpole Swingup: baseline (3 seeds), trained dt_emb (3 seeds), no dt_emb (3 seeds)\n- Hopper Hop: 3 seeds × 3 configs\n- RSSM fidelity analysis, actor entropy analysis, value distribution analysis, cosine similarity analysis\n- NVE threshold sensitivity (AUROC computation)\n- TCN: Walker σ=0.1/0.2/0.5, Cheetah σ=0.5, PickCube σ=0.2\n- Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\n\nThe paper appears to cover most of these experiments. Some TCN results (s1 σ=0.2, Cheetah σ=0.5, Walker σ=0.1, PickCube) are labeled as \"running — median prediction\" (i.e., projected, not actual).\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the factorial design well-constructed? Are the controls appropriate? Is the NVE metric well-defined and meaningful?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are there enough seeds? Is statistical analysis sufficient? Are the claimed results well-supported?\n3. **Completeness**: Does the paper cover enough experiments from the design? Are there obvious missing experiments?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Limitations**: Are limitations honestly reported?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, specific)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Recommendation: Accept / Borderline / Reject\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":665,"cache_read_input_tokens":73451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":665},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"39f1e178-a592-4272-bca1-185774f5c56a"}
