{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014fMo32pc3apjPEjhAcdPF1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\tWe organize results around seven findings (F1--F7) that establish a causal chain from trained temporal conditioning to pathological value dynamics. All experiments use Walker Walk (DMC) with DreamerV3; treatments run to 105K steps and the baseline to 505K steps. We report evaluation returns (mean over 10 episodes) and the normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\\nve$, \\S\\ref{sec:metrics}).\n5\t\n6\t%% ============================================================\n7\t%% TABLE 1: Main results summary\n8\t%% ============================================================\n9\t\n10\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n11\t\\centering\n12\t\\caption{Key configurations and outcomes. NVE reports peak $\\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\\S\\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded. Complete results for all 31 configurations in Table~\\ref{tab:complete_results}.}\n13\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n14\t\\footnotesize\n15\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n16\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}\n17\t\\toprule\n18\t\\textbf{Exp} & \\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{dt\\_emb} & \\textbf{Sub} & \\textbf{H} & \\textbf{Eval@final} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n19\t\\midrule\n20\t001 & Baseline ($\\dt{=}1$) & --- & --- & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n21\t\\midrule\n22\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n23\t008d & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\\times$ & Full collapse \\\\\n24\t008f & No dt\\_emb (s1) & --- & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.97$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n25\t008a & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.93$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n26\t015b & Frozen emb & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 204.2 @105K & 1.45$\\times$ & Grad.\\ starvation \\\\\n27\t022b & Frozen emb + Gumbel gate & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 300.7 @105K & 0.63$\\times$ & No CIVO \\\\\n28\t\\midrule\n29\t\\multicolumn{8}{l}{\\textit{Cross-environment validation}} \\\\\n30\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n31\t010b & Cheetah Run & trn & ON & 8 & 51.7 @105K & 8.27$\\times$ & Collapsed \\\\\n32\t019b & PickCube-v1 & trn & ON & 8 & 26.64 @200K & 3.78$\\times$$^c$ & Transient onset \\\\\n33\t\\bottomrule\n34\t\\end{tabular}\n35\t\\vspace{1pt}\n36\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize\n37\ttrn = trained ($d{=}32$, subsample ON);\n38\texp$^a$ = trained, subsample OFF;\n39\tfrz$^b$ = frozen ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False});\n40\t--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\\\\n41\t$^c$ NVE normalized against exp\\_019a\\_v2 (vanilla DreamerV3 on PickCube-v1).\\par}\n42\t\\end{table*}\n43\t\n44\t%% ============================================================\n45\t%% F1: All dt>1 degrade\n46\t%% ============================================================\n47\t\n48\t\\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}\n49\t\\label{sec:f1}\n50\t\n51\tEvery configuration that introduces $\\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\\%$ of baseline); fixed $\\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\\%$).\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n54\t\\centering\n55\t\\includegraphics[width=0.65\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}\n56\t\\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}\n57\t\\label{fig:learning_curves}\n58\t\\end{figure}\n59\t\n60\t%% ============================================================\n61\t%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x\n62\t%% ============================================================\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Triggers Value Overestimation}\n65\t\\label{sec:f2}\n66\t\n67\tThe factorial design isolates trained $\\dt$-embedding as the causal trigger. Comparing exp\\_008d (trained, subsample ON) against exp\\_008f (no embedding, subsample ON): 008d reaches $\\nve = 4.12\\times$ and collapses (eval 82.8), while 008f maintains $\\nve \\leq 0.97\\times$ (eval 503.0). The $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold (35K+ window) correctly classifies 27 of 29 experimental configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000; Figure~\\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}; per-threshold breakdown in Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_sensitivity}).\n68\t\n69\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n70\t\\centering\n71\t\\includegraphics[width=0.65\\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}\n72\t\\caption{NVE trajectories (mean $\\pm$ seed range). Trained $\\dt$-embedding (red, Walker Walk, $n{=}3$ seeds plotted; seed~4 data in Table~\\ref{tab:complete_results}) exceeds the $1.5\\times$ threshold (dashed) in all seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\\times$. Three trajectory types emerge: early collapse (seed~0), late-onset collapse (seed~3), and self-correction (seed~2). Dotted line marks the 35K diagnostic window onset.}\n73\t\\label{fig:nve_trajectory}\n74\t\\end{figure}\n75\t\n76\tThe trained $\\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in all four Walker Walk seeds (peak NVE: $4.62\\times$, $4.12\\times$, $1.62\\times$, $1.53\\times$), universally degrading performance to below 30\\% of baseline (mean eval 170.6). Downstream trajectories diverge: two seeds undergo full collapse, one exhibits late-onset collapse still declining at 105K, and one self-corrects to a stable but degraded state (29.3\\% of baseline). Combined with Cheetah Run (\\S\\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset is universal across all six tested seeds ($n{=}6$, two environments) with 5/6 progressing toward collapse (Appendix~\\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).\n77\t\n78\t%% ============================================================\n79\t%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)\n80\t%% ============================================================\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}\n83\t\\label{sec:f3f5_summary}\n84\t\n85\tTrained $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \\emph{jointly necessary} for CIVO. Neither factor alone triggers pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, 3 seeds, peak NVE $0.83$--$0.93\\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, 3 seeds, peak NVE $\\leq 1.41\\times$) both remain below the $1.5\\times$ threshold. Cross-environment replication on Cheetah Run (2 seeds, peak NVE $1.08$--$1.29\\times$ at 35K, resolving to $< 0.5\\times$ by 105K) confirms the protective effect generalizes. Frozen embedding (015b) peaks at $1.45\\times$ but self-corrects to $0.42\\times$; the frozen-embedding NVE oscillates and decays, contrasting with the monotonic escalation under trained conditioning. Adding a Gumbel-Softmax gate to the frozen embedding (022b, NVE $0.63\\times$) further confirms that trainability, not temporal signal availability, is the necessary condition (Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). Degradation scales monotonically with $\\dt$ ($-47\\%$ at $\\dt{=}2$, $-81\\%$ at $\\dt{=}4$; Figure~\\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).\n86\t\n87\t%% ============================================================\n88\t%% F6--F7: Interventions and cross-environment\n89\t%% ============================================================\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions and Multiple Pathways}\n92\t\\label{sec:f6}\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{F6: $\\dt{=}1$ imagination does not prevent all collapse.}\n95\tExp\\_008e\\_v3 restricts imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Two of four seeds collapse (50\\%) via qualitatively distinct mechanisms: Pathway~A (seed~3, CIVO with entropy decrease, $\\nve = 4.02\\times$, eval 92.7) and Pathway~B (seed~2, entropy \\emph{increase}, $\\nve = 3.78\\times$, eval 137.0). The remaining seeds are healthy (eval 870--899). The bimodal outcome reveals that the trained embedding creates vulnerability through three independent mechanism families encompassing seven observed failure and degradation patterns (Table~\\ref{tab:failure_modes}; Figure~\\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}; Appendix~\\ref{sec:pathways}). Pathway~B produces one of two non-CIVO false positives in the expanded NVE classification ($n{=}29$; the second arises from intervention side effects, Appendix~\\ref{sec:roc_analysis}).\n96\t\n97\t\\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}\n98\tFull-SGS (exp\\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, 49\\% of baseline; peak NVE reduced from $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$) but remains CIVO-positive by the $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold, while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8); CIVO elimination rate is 0/2 (Figure~\\ref{fig:civo_decomposition}; Appendix~\\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Gradient decomposition reveals the reward head contributes 38--49\\% of $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\dt}$ yet reward-only SGS (016e) fails entirely (NVE $10.42\\times$), while the observation decoder (16--22\\%) carries the causally critical $\\dt$-specific signal. Cross-environment validation on Cheetah Run (010b, 2 seeds) confirms CIVO generalization (peak NVE $8.27\\times$, $108.52\\times$; collapse to eval 51.7, 0.83). Full-SGS on Cheetah (exp\\_021) produces transient self-correcting CIVO (peak NVE $9.94\\times$, subsiding to $0.34\\times$ by 105K; eval 221.5); this trajectory is not attributable to SGS intervention, yielding 0/3 CIVO elimination across environments. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO entirely on Cheetah (024b, 2 seeds, NVE $< 0.2\\times$), replicating the Walker causal control (008f).\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS confirms pathway redundancy.}\n101\tObs-decoder-only SGS (exp\\_025/026) achieves 1/6 CIVO prevention across environments (Table~\\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}): 1/4 on Walker (three distinct collapse trajectories including late-onset rebound to $\\nve = 5.47\\times$) and 0/2 on Cheetah ($\\nve = 21.9\\times$, $30.2\\times$). The 1/6 rate is not meaningfully different from full-pathway SGS (0/3), confirming that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels: blocking any single pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (per-seed trajectories in Appendix~\\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).\n102\t\n103\t\\begin{table}[t]\n104\t\\centering\n105\t\\caption{Intervention hierarchy. Prevention = seeds with peak NVE $< 1.5\\times$ (35K+). Interventions span three levels; even representation-level TCN achieves only environment-specific partial mitigation.}\n106\t\\label{tab:intervention_hierarchy}\n107\t\\footnotesize\n108\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n109\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n110\t\\toprule\n111\t\\textbf{Level} & \\textbf{Intervention} & \\textbf{Seeds} & \\textbf{Prev.} & \\textbf{Rate} \\\\\n112\t\\midrule\n113\t--- & Untreated & 6 & 0/6 & 0\\% \\\\\n114\t\\midrule\n115\tGradient & Reward-only SGS & 2 & 0/2 & 0\\% \\\\\n116\tGradient & Full-pathway SGS & 3 & 0/3 & 0\\% \\\\\n117\tGradient & Obs-decoder SGS & 6 & 1/6 & 17\\% \\\\\n118\t\\midrule\n119\tOutput & RAVE ($c{=}2.0$) & 1 & 0/1 & 0\\% \\\\\n120\tOutput & RAVE ($c{=}1.5$) & 1 & 0/1 & 0\\% \\\\\n121\t\\midrule\n122\tRepr. & TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.5$) & 3 & 0/3 & 0\\% \\\\\n123\tRepr. & TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) & 4$^\\dagger$ & 2/4 & 50\\% \\\\\n124\t\\midrule\n125\t--- & Embedding removed & 5 & 5/5 & 100\\% \\\\\n126\t\\bottomrule\n127\t\\end{tabular}\n128\t\\vspace{1pt}\n129\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize $^\\dagger$Walker 2/2 prevented (eval 45--91\\% baseline); Cheetah 0/1, PickCube 0/1.\\par}\n130\t\\end{table}\n131\t\n132\t%% ============================================================\n133\t%% TABLE 2: RSSM Fidelity\n134\t%% ============================================================\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{table}[t]\n137\t\\centering\n138\t\\caption{RSSM fidelity at $\\dt{=}1$ vs.\\ $\\dt{=}2$. Trained embedding (008d) produces $2.78\\times$ reward gap; removing it (008f) eliminates the gap but degrades absolute prediction.}\n139\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n140\t\\footnotesize\n141\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n142\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n143\t\\toprule\n144\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{obs\\_MSE}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{reward\\_MAE}} \\\\\n145\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n146\t\\textbf{Config} & $\\dt{=}1$ & $\\dt{=}2$ & Gap & $\\dt{=}1$ & $\\dt{=}2$ & Gap \\\\\n147\t\\midrule\n148\tBaseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n149\t008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & 2.78$\\times$ \\\\\n150\t008f (no emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n151\t\\bottomrule\n152\t\\end{tabular}\n153\t\\end{table}\n154\t\n155\tThe trained embedding produces a $2.78\\times$ reward prediction gap between $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates.\n156\t\n157\t%% ============================================================\n158\t%% Forward-pass interventions\n159\t%% ============================================================\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}\n162\t\\label{sec:forward_results}\n163\t\n164\tThe SGS results establish gradient stopping as categorically insufficient. We test two forward-pass alternatives (Table~\\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). \\textbf{RAVE} (value-level capping) fails at both ceiling values: the EMA anchor tracks biased imagined values during CIVO onset, rendering the ceiling ineffective. \\textbf{TCN} (representation-level noise) on Walker Walk shows dose-dependent behavior: $\\sigma{=}0.5$ produces oscillatory CIVO in both seeds (eval $\\leq 40\\%$), while $\\sigma{=}0.2$ consistently prevents CIVO (2/2 seeds, NVE $< 1.0\\times$) but with high eval variance---seed~0 reaches $91\\%$ of baseline (821.1 at 175K), while seed~1 reaches only $45\\%$ (eval 412). TCN fails on Cheetah Run ($\\sigma{=}0.2, 0.5$) and PickCube-v1 ($\\sigma{=}0.2$); detailed trajectories including a novel transient-spike failure mode are in Appendix~\\ref{sec:tcn_cross_env}. Among the three levels tested, only representation-level noise produced any configuration with NVE $< 1.0\\times$ (2/8, both Walker $\\sigma{=}0.2$), underscoring that CIVO is not a simple regularization deficiency but a structural vulnerability of imagination-based bootstrapping with temporal conditioning.\n165\t\n166\t%% ============================================================\n167\t%% F8: Online CIVO Detection\n168\t%% ============================================================\n169\t\n170\t\\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}\n171\t\\label{sec:detector}\n172\t\n173\tWe derive a lightweight online detector that monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth characteristic of CIVO's positive feedback loop. Applied to 14 runs across three environments (DMC Walker Walk, DMC Cheetah Run, ManiSkill3 PickCube-v1), the detector achieves TPR $= 5/5$, FPR $= 2/9$, with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases. Algorithm specification, per-run validation, and threshold sensitivity analysis are in Appendix~\\ref{sec:detector_appendix}.\n174\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"7fa5ad06-647c-4d7c-8d00-807955cbf11f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:01:52.622Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018hAuArd4FpXo8V7tJ6X1H2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GWTQaJ9g7hUB9EQue15UDr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/methodology.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21870,"cache_read_input_tokens":19390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21870,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"6d5de43e-a57c-4b2c-8eae-d8cd544fc393"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GWTQaJ9g7hUB9EQue15UDr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n2\t\\label{sec:methodology}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Base Architecture}\n5\t\\label{sec:base_arch}\n6\t\n7\tAll experiments build on DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.\n8\t\n9\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Mechanism}\n10\t\\label{sec:variable_rate}\n11\t\n12\tWe introduce three orthogonal architectural modifications that enable variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3. Each can be independently toggled to isolate its contribution.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}\n15\tA learned embedding $e(\\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step to condition state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:\n16\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n17\t\\item \\textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, so the RSSM can co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.\n18\t\\item \\textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\\texttt{requires\\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.\n19\t\\item \\textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.\n20\t\\end{itemize}\n21\tWe further distinguish \\textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \\textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification (trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed) is the key causal structure of our factorial design.\n22\t\n23\t\\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}\n24\tWhen enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.\n25\t\n26\t\\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}\n27\tThe imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. The parameter \\texttt{gate\\_fixed\\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$); this separates the effect of $\\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.\n28\t\n29\t\\subsection{Factorial Design}\n30\t\\label{sec:factorial}\n31\t\n32\tWe adopt a factorial ablation across four factors: $\\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\\dt{=}1$, $2$, $4$). The design (12 conditions, Table~\\ref{tab:factorial_design} in Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) isolates trained $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as orthogonal factors. Key configurations use 4 seeds; following \\citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories.\n33\t\n34\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n35\t\\label{sec:metrics}\n36\t\n37\t\\paragraph{Normalized value-to-eval ratio ($\\nve$).}\n38\tWe define $\\nve_t = (v/e)_{\\text{config},t} / (v/e)_{\\text{baseline},t}$, where $v/e$ is the critic's mean imagined value divided by mean evaluation return, and the baseline is the highest-performing seed (exp\\_001, seed~1, 505K steps). We restrict to a \\textbf{35K+ window} to avoid early-training inflation. The $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold achieves 27/29 accuracy (AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000; Appendix~\\ref{sec:nve_robustness}).\n39\t\n40\t\\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}\n41\t\\label{sec:sgs_method}\n42\t\n43\tTo identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \\textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): $e_{\\Delta t}$ is detached via $\\operatorname{sg}(\\cdot)$ before selected prediction heads during the backward pass, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\\Delta t}$ while preserving its forward-pass contribution. We test three variants: reward-only (exp\\_016e), full-pathway (exp\\_018, all non-KL gradients blocked), and obs-decoder-only (exp\\_025/026). Gradient decomposition using $\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L}_i$ quantifies each pathway's contribution (Appendix~\\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}).\n44\t\n45\t\\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}\n46\t\\label{sec:forward_pass_method}\n47\t\n48\tWe test two forward-pass interventions as representation- and output-level probes: \\textbf{TCN} adds Gaussian noise $\\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2)$ to $e_{\\Delta t}$ during imagination ($\\sigma \\in \\{0.5, 0.2\\}$, three environments); \\textbf{RAVE} clips imagined values exceeding an EMA anchor ($c \\in \\{2.0, 1.5\\}$).\n49\t\n50\t\\subsection{Environment and Training Details}\n51\t\\label{sec:env_details}\n52\t\n53\tAll experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from the DeepMind Control Suite (DMC; \\citealt{tassa2018dmcontrol}) with proprioceptive observations, chosen for their contrasting dynamics (6-joint biped vs.\\ 2-joint runner). The baseline runs for 500K steps (peak return 970.4); treatments run for 105K steps, sufficient to observe collapse onset. All hyperparameters follow the DreamerV3 defaults \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} without task-specific tuning ($d{=}32$ temporal embedding; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\\ref{sec:hyperparameters}). Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (${\\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\\footnote{\\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described in \\S\\ref{sec:variable_rate}.\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"e1b470a2-c279-4585-b175-27c1f0941d9a","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:01:53.001Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018hAuArd4FpXo8V7tJ6X1H2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019fM4sjHXLTddpVwL5r3CsU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21870,"cache_read_input_tokens":19390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21870,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"9b2658e1-c3fc-4063-a0b4-8e0c2d39bd75"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019fM4sjHXLTddpVwL5r3CsU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in world-model RL.}\n5\tThe options framework \\citep{sutton1999options} and action-repetition methods \\citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl} achieve temporal abstraction via real transitions. The Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3, hafner2025dreamerv4} trains policies in imagination at fixed $\\dt{=}1$; hierarchical variants \\citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros, li2025lsimagine} maintain separate dynamics per scale. TAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains; the critical distinction is that MPC re-plans from real states, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation and model fidelity.}\n8\tMaximization bias \\citep{thrun1993issues} has been addressed by Double Q-learning \\citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \\citep{fujimoto2018td3}, and ensemble methods \\citep{chen2021redq}; in model-based settings, MBPO \\citep{janner2019mbpo} and MOPO \\citep{yu2020mopo} bound rollout error. \\citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints. CIVO is mechanistically distinct: the amplifier is the world model's co-adaptation with a learned temporal embedding, producing systematic reward-prediction bias at $\\dt > 1$. Compounding error in multi-step rollouts \\citep{lambert2020objective, asadi2019combating} exacerbates this bias; our gradient decomposition confirms the observation decoder channel as the carrier of $\\dt$-specific information. Our factorial diagnostic follows \\citet{henderson2018deep} in systematically isolating design-choice effects.\n9\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"96abd41f-88b5-4cf0-ba77-6412cdd73662","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:01:53.467Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GdpanDw3q6LwQtD9jHwCTU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UYAhAGN6MCXs6YiBXL2zzL","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 reacts to R1's review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** in a roundtable discussion of a CoRL 2026 paper: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3.\"\n\nYou have already submitted your review. Now you are reading R1's review. Respond with:\n1. Points of **agreement** with R1\n2. Points of **disagreement** with R1 (with reasoning)\n3. Whether R1's points change your assessment — and if so, how\n4. Your **updated scores** (or confirmation that you maintain your original scores)\n\n## Your Original Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n- Overall: 4/10 (Reject)\n- Key strengths: MPC vs imagination insight, rigorous factorial design, gradient magnitude vs causal importance, comprehensive intervention hierarchy, transparent reporting, dual-pathway finding\n- Key weaknesses: CIVO is instantiation of known MBRL failure modes, NVE trivially constructed/in-sample validation, TCN not novel and mostly fails, no standard overestimation countermeasures tested, scoped only to DreamerV3/RSSM on DMC, weak CoRL relevance, inadequate statistical power, dual-pathway mechanistically shallow, thin reference list\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n**Overall: 5/10 (Borderline, leaning reject)**\n\n**R1 Strengths identified:**\n- Well-constructed factorial design (textbook causal isolation)\n- Gradient decomposition reveals non-obvious finding (magnitude ≠ causal importance)\n- NVE well-defined and statistically validated (AUROC=0.971, n=29, Youden-optimal threshold)\n- Multi-seed replication with per-seed trajectories\n- Honest limitation reporting\n- Random-policy decomposition separating Stage 1 (36%) and Stage 2 (64%)\n- Systematic intervention hierarchy\n\n**R1 Weaknesses identified:**\n- R1 initially claimed \"only 2 DMC environments in main causal analysis\" — BUT CORRECTED: the paper actually DOES include Cartpole Swingup (Table 10) and Hopper Hop (Table 9) with baselines and CIVO results across 4 environments. Table 2 shows all 4 environments' CIVO severity.\n- Seed counts uneven and sometimes insufficient (frozen emb 1 seed, RAVE 1 seed, TCN Walker 2 seeds, etc.)\n- NVE uses single baseline seed for normalization; AUROC drops from 0.933 to 0.867 under 4-seed normalization\n- Treatment runs 105K vs baseline 505K (asymmetric)\n- Paper is overwhelmingly a negative result\n- RSSM fidelity analysis at single checkpoint\n- No error bars or CIs on evaluation returns\n- Online detector has 4 hand-tuned thresholds calibrated on n=14 runs (FPR 22%)\n\n**R1 Questions:**\n1. Where are Cartpole/Hopper results? (R1 noted they actually ARE in the paper)\n2. NVE threshold transferability to different reward scales?\n3. Obs-decoder gradients: how does 1/6 prevention support \"causally critical\" claim?\n4. TCN sigma calibration: principled way to predict sigma for new environment?\n5. Dual-pathway: reconcile 008e_v3 50% collapse with 008a 3/3 healthy\n6. Pixel observations testing?\n\n**R1 Missing experiments:**\n1. Cartpole/Hopper results (actually present)\n2. Temporal profile of RSSM fidelity gap\n3. Pixel-observation experiments\n4. Longer training runs for treatment conditions\n5. Within-seed evaluation CIs\n6. Standard value overestimation mitigation (Double Q, ensemble critics)\n7. Embedding dimension ablation\n\n**R1 Recommendation: Borderline (leaning reject)**\n\n## Important Correction\nNOTE: R1 initially stated Cartpole and Hopper results were absent, but then corrected this — the paper DOES include Table 2 (all 4 environments CIVO severity), Table 9 (Hopper Hop 3-config summary), and Table 10 (Cartpole Swingup 3-seed trained dt_emb results). The paper covers 4 DMC environments, not 2.\n\n## Your Task\nReact to R1's review. Be specific. In particular, re-evaluate your claim about \"only 2 environments\" in light of the correction that all 4 environments are present. Then provide updated scores or confirm originals.\n\nFormat:\n```\n## R2 Reaction to R1\n\n### Agreements\n\n### Disagreements\n\n### Impact on My Assessment\n\n### Updated Scores (or confirmation of original)\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11643,"cache_read_input_tokens":74116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11643},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"83a7d449-1474-4a10-a246-59339553356a"}
