t of the correction that all 4 environments are present. Then provide updated scores or confirm originals.\n\nFormat:\n```\n## R2 Reaction to R1\n\n### Agreements\n\n### Disagreements\n\n### Impact on My Assessment\n\n### Updated Scores (or confirmation of original)\n```\n","uuid":"a5012f8b-3592-46c1-abc1-bb14262f492b","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** in a roundtable discussion of a CoRL 2026 paper: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3.\"\n\nYou have already submitted your review. Now you are reading R1's review. Respond with:\n1. Points of **agreement** with R1\n2. Points of **disagreement** with R1 (with reasoning)\n3. Whether R1's points change your assessment — and if so, how\n4. Your **updated scores** (or confirmation that you maintain your original scores)\n\n## Your Original Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n- Overall: 4/10 (Reject)\n- Key strengths: MPC vs imagination insight, rigorous factorial design, gradient magnitude vs causal importance, comprehensive intervention hierarchy, transparent reporting, dual-pathway finding\n- Key weaknesses: CIVO is instantiation of known MBRL failure modes, NVE trivially constructed/in-sample validation, TCN not novel and mostly fails, no standard overestimation countermeasures tested, scoped only to DreamerV3/RSSM on DMC, weak CoRL relevance, inadequate statistical power, dual-pathway mechanistically shallow, thin reference list\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n**Overall: 5/10 (Borderline, leaning reject)**\n\n**R1 Strengths identified:**\n- Well-constructed factorial design (textbook causal isolation)\n- Gradient decomposition reveals non-obvious finding (magnitude ≠ causal importance)\n- NVE well-defined and statistically validated (AUROC=0.971, n=29, Youden-optimal threshold)\n- Multi-seed replication with per-seed trajectories\n- Honest limitation reporting\n- Random-policy decomposition separating Stage 1 (36%) and Stage 2 (64%)\n- Systematic intervention hierarchy\n\n**R1 Weaknesses identified:**\n- R1 initially claimed \"only 2 DMC environments in main causal analysis\" — BUT CORRECTED: the paper actually DOES include Cartpole Swingup (Table 10) and Hopper Hop (Table 9) with baselines and CIVO results across 4 environments. Table 2 shows all 4 environments' CIVO severity.\n- Seed counts uneven and sometimes insufficient (frozen emb 1 seed, RAVE 1 seed, TCN Walker 2 seeds, etc.)\n- NVE uses single baseline seed for normalization; AUROC drops from 0.933 to 0.867 under 4-seed normalization\n- Treatment runs 105K vs baseline 505K (asymmetric)\n- Paper is overwhelmingly a negative result\n- RSSM fidelity analysis at single checkpoint\n- No error bars or CIs on evaluation returns\n- Online detector has 4 hand-tuned thresholds calibrated on n=14 runs (FPR 22%)\n\n**R1 Questions:**\n1. Where are Cartpole/Hopper results? (R1 noted they actually ARE in the paper)\n2. NVE threshold transferability to different reward scales?\n3. Obs-decoder gradients: how does 1/6 prevention support \"causally critical\" claim?\n4. TCN sigma calibration: principled way to predict sigma for new environment?\n5. Dual-pathway: reconcile 008e_v3 50% collapse with 008a 3/3 healthy\n6. Pixel observations testing?\n\n**R1 Missing experiments:**\n1. Cartpole/Hopper results (actually present)\n2. Temporal profile of RSSM fidelity gap\n3. Pixel-observation experiments\n4. Longer training runs for treatment conditions\n5. Within-seed evaluation CIs\n6. Standard value overestimation mitigation (Double Q, ensemble critics)\n7. Embedding dimension ablation\n\n**R1 Recommendation: Borderline (leaning reject)**\n\n## Important Correction\nNOTE: R1 initially stated Cartpole and Hopper results were absent, but then corrected this — the paper DOES include Table 2 (all 4 environments CIVO severity), Table 9 (Hopper Hop 3-config summary), and Table 10 (Cartpole Swingup 3-seed trained dt_emb results). The paper covers 4 DMC environments, not 2.\n\n## Your Task\nReact to R1's review. Be specific. In particular, re-evaluate your claim about \"only 2 environments\" in light of the correction that all 4 environments are present. Then provide updated scores or confirm originals.\n\nFormat:\n```\n## R2 Reaction to R1\n\n### Agreements\n\n### Disagreements\n\n### Impact on My Assessment\n\n### Updated Scores (or confirmation of original)\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYAhAGN6MCXs6YiBXL2zzL","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"204d9329-95f4-49f5-931c-aec596493147","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:01:57.551Z"}
