{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B5Cs7z5SRe8hjV9tQuePgD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tTemporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination promises to extend the effective planning horizon of world-model reinforcement learning, and $\\Delta t$-conditioning has proven effective in model-predictive control (MPC) methods. We find that this approach cannot be directly transferred to imagination-based actor-critic training: every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 we test degrades performance. Through factorial diagnostics on two continuous-control tasks (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run), we identify the combination of trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the reward head of the recurrent state-space model (RSSM) to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, universally degrading performance to below 30\\% of baseline across all six tested seeds in two environments ($n{=}6$). Gradient decomposition reveals that the observation decoder channel, not the reward head, carries the critical $\\Delta t$-specific spatial information that drives the fidelity gap. Removing multi-rate data exposure suppresses CIVO entirely (5 seeds, all below the $1.5\\times$ diagnostic threshold); full-pathway gradient stopping reduces CIVO severity in one of two Walker seeds (peak NVE $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$) but eliminates it in neither (0/3 across environments), exposing mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation. A lightweight online detector identifies all five CIVO cases (100\\% sensitivity) with advance warning before performance degradation in two cases, enabling early intervention without a reference baseline. The contrast with MPC methods identifies the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping versus re-planning) as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics. Targeted gradient stopping on the identified obs-decoder pathway fails to prevent CIVO (1/6 seeds across two environments), confirming that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels that no single-pathway intervention addresses. A three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation) reveals that even representation-level noise injection (TCN) achieves only partial, environment-specific mitigation: TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) prevents CIVO on Walker Walk (2/2 seeds, NVE $< 1.0\\times$) but with high eval variance (45--91\\% of baseline), and fails entirely on Cheetah Run and PickCube-v1, further establishing CIVO as a fundamental vulnerability of imagination-based $\\Delta t$-conditioning.\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"0f6cbc4c-e502-4788-9d3c-c441fb1e7c50","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:02:03.874Z"}
