{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lxs8KzEnjpwGspA1CnvvxU","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:7:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:10:TAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} succeeds with $\\dt$-conditioning in TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} because MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic. In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination: the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$ produces biased value estimates that the actor exploits, closing a positive feedback loop with no MPC counterpart (Appendix~\\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:5:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:8:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/methodology.tex:7:All experiments build on DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.\ndocs/paper/sections/methodology.tex:32:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/methodology.tex:53:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:365:This mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional}, but differs in origin: delusional bias arises from policy-class constraints, while CIVO is model-induced: the reward head's co-adaptation with the temporal embedding produces systematically upward-biased predictions at $\\dt > 1$.  The consequence (policy exploitation of overestimated regions) is similar, but the causal pathway is distinct.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:470:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:503:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:525:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:4:TAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's model-predictive control (MPC) framework \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:6:[Omitted long matching line]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6ZrXBjcFR7Kow2tSrSgdr","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"13e9686e-1213-4faf-8596-e77123e1dddc","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:02:20.107Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GHG55wEAwv6JWTfb4SqAMS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\n15\t\\definecolor{civo}{RGB}{180,40,40}\n16\t\\definecolor{healthy}{RGB}{40,120,40}\n17\t\\definecolor{partial}{RGB}{180,140,30}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Diagnosing and Mitigating Value Overestimation\\\\from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous Authors}\n22\t\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t%% ============================================================\n30\t%%  ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tTemporal abstraction---executing actions at variable rates---is a long-sought capability in reinforcement learning, yet integrating it into world-model agents remains poorly understood.\n34\tWe present a systematic diagnostic study of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying a failure mode we term \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO): when a trained temporal-step embedding (\\texttt{dt\\_embedding}) conditions the world model, the critic systematically overestimates values for imagined states at $dt>1$, triggering a positive feedback loop that collapses the policy.\n35\tThrough factorial experiments spanning 4 environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop), 30+ configurations, and 60+ seeds, we establish that\n36\t(i)~a normalized value-to-evaluation ratio (NVE) $\\geq 1.5\\times$ at 35K+ steps reliably diagnoses CIVO (AUROC\\,=\\,0.969, 26/26 classification across 4 environments),\n37\t(ii)~CIVO arises from forward-pass representational damage, not gradient contamination---all backward-pass (selective gradient stopping) interventions fail (1/17 seeds, 3 variants, 2 environments),\n38\tand (iii)~a forward-pass intervention, \\emph{Temporal Conditioning Noise} (TCN), which injects Gaussian noise into the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} during imagination, successfully prevents CIVO while retaining 90\\% baseline performance (Walker, $\\sigma=0.2$) and reduces NVE from 8--108$\\times$ to 1.5--4$\\times$ (Cheetah, $\\sigma=0.5$).\n39\tA dose-response relationship between untreated CIVO severity and required noise magnitude provides additional mechanistic insight.\n40\tAll claims are scoped to DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%% ============================================================\n44\t%%  1. INTRODUCTION\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\n48\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and act at multiple time scales---is a foundational desideratum in reinforcement learning \\cite{sutton1999between,precup2000temporal}.\n49\tIn model-based RL, world models offer a natural substrate: by conditioning imagination on a temporal step size $dt$, an agent can simulate future trajectories at variable rates without modifying environment interaction.\n50\tRecent successes with DreamerV3 \\cite{hafner2023mastering} have made latent-dynamics world models the de facto platform for such experiments.\n51\t\n52\tYet a persistent puzzle emerges: every attempt to equip DreamerV3 with variable-rate imagination---whether via learned gating, fixed $dt>1$, or temporal subsampling---degrades asymptotic performance, often catastrophically.\n53\tThis stands in contrast to the success of temporal conditioning in model-predictive control (MPC) settings, where TAWM \\cite{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates effective variable-rate planning with TD-MPC2.\n54\tWhy does temporal abstraction succeed in MPC but fail in imagination-based actor-critic?\n55\t\n56\tThis paper provides a systematic, causally controlled answer.\n57\tThrough factorial experiments spanning 4 DeepMind Control Suite environments, 30+ configurations, and 60+ seeds, we identify the root cause: a failure mode we term \\textbf{conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO)}.\n58\tWhen a trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} conditions the RSSM world model, the critic exploits precise but inaccurate temporal conditioning to systematically overestimate values at $dt>1$, triggering a positive feedback loop (overestimation $\\to$ actor exploitation $\\to$ distribution shift $\\to$ worse predictions $\\to$ greater overestimation) that collapses the policy.\n59\t\n60\tOur investigation proceeds in three stages:\n61\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Diagnosis} (C1--C5): We isolate trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} as the necessary and sufficient condition for CIVO through controlled ablations, establishing a diagnostic threshold (NVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$) with near-perfect classification across environments.\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanism} (C6--C7): We show CIVO operates through forward-pass representational damage, not gradient contamination. All three backward-pass interventions (selective gradient stopping) fail across 17 seeds and 2 environments, while the trained embedding creates vulnerability through at least two distinct pathways.\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Mitigation} (C8): Guided by the forward-pass diagnosis, we propose \\emph{Temporal Conditioning Noise} (TCN), which injects calibrated Gaussian noise into the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} during imagination. TCN prevents CIVO in Walker ($\\sigma=0.2$, NVE\\,$<$\\,1.0$\\times$) and substantially reduces it in the more severe Cheetah environment ($\\sigma=0.5$, NVE from 8--108$\\times$ to 1.5--4$\\times$), with a dose-response relationship linking noise magnitude to untreated severity.\n65\t\\end{enumerate}\n66\t\n67\tAll claims are scoped to DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model.\n68\tGeneralizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n69\t\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t%%  2. BACKGROUND & RELATED WORK\n72\t%% ============================================================\n73\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n76\tThe options framework \\cite{sutton1999between,precup2000temporal} formalizes temporal abstraction as policies over variable-length sub-routines.\n77\tFiGAR \\cite{sharma2017learning} learns action repetition counts.\n78\tIn model-free settings, simple action repeat \\cite{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic} is the most common form.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n81\tThe Dreamer family \\cite{hafner2020dream,hafner2021mastering,hafner2023mastering} learns latent-dynamics models (RSSM) for imagination-based policy optimization.\n82\tIRIS \\cite{micheli2023transformers} and TWM \\cite{robine2023transformer} use transformer world models.\n83\tThese methods train actor and critic entirely within imagined trajectories.\n84\t\n85\t\\paragraph{Variable-rate planning in world models.}\n86\tTAWM \\cite{nhu2025tawm} successfully conditions TD-MPC2's world model on temporal step size for variable-rate MPC planning.\n87\tThe key architectural difference is that MPC re-plans from real environment states at each step, while imagination-based actor-critic bootstraps from imagined states---creating the positive feedback loop that enables CIVO.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n90\tModel exploitation---where the policy exploits inaccuracies in the learned model---is a known challenge \\cite{janner2019trust}.\n91\tLu et al.~\\cite{lu2018non} identify ``delusional bias'' from policy-class constraints.\n92\tOur CIVO mechanism is distinct: it arises from \\emph{conditioning-induced} exploitation of temporal embedding inaccuracies, not from policy constraints or general model error.\n93\t\n94\t%% ============================================================\n95\t%%  3. METHODOLOGY\n96\t%% ============================================================\n97\t\\section{Methodology}\n98\t\n99\t\\subsection{DreamerV3 with Variable-Rate Imagination}\n100\t\n101\tWe extend DreamerV3 \\cite{hafner2023mastering} with temporal abstraction components:\n102\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n103\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: Training data includes transitions at $dt=1$ and $dt>1$ (obtained by skipping intermediate frames).\n104\t    \\item \\textbf{dt\\_embedding}: A learned embedding $e_{dt} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ conditions the RSSM, reward head, and observation decoder on the temporal step size.\n105\t    \\item \\textbf{Variable-rate imagination}: During actor-critic training, imagined rollouts can use $dt>1$ steps.\n106\t\\end{itemize}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}\n109\t\n110\tWe use a controlled factorial design varying three factors:\n111\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n112\t    \\item \\textbf{dt\\_embedding state}: trained, untrained/unexposed (present but never sees $dt>1$ data), frozen (random fixed), or absent ($\\text{dim}=0$).\n113\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: ON (training data contains $dt>1$ transitions) or OFF.\n114\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination horizon}: $H=8$ or $H=15$; imagination dt: $dt=1$ or $dt=2$.\n115\t\\end{enumerate}\n116\tExperiments span 4 DMC environments: Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop.\n117\t\n118\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metric: Normalized Value-to-Evaluation Ratio (NVE)}\n119\t\n120\tWe define the \\emph{normalized value-to-evaluation ratio}:\n121\t\\begin{equation}\n122\t    \\text{NVE}(t) = \\frac{V(t) / E(t)}{V_{\\text{base}}(t) / E_{\\text{base}}(t)},\n123\t\\end{equation}\n124\twhere $V(t)$ is the mean predicted value, $E(t)$ is the mean evaluation return, and subscript ``base'' denotes the $dt=1$ baseline at the same training step.\n125\tNVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$ at $t \\geq 35$K indicates pathological overestimation; NVE\\,$<$\\,1.0$\\times$ indicates value suppression.\n126\tThe 35K lower bound ensures the baseline value-to-evaluation ratio has stabilized (at 25K, baseline $V/E$ is only 0.087, causing ratio instability).\n127\t\n128\t%% ============================================================\n129\t%%  4. RESULTS\n130\t%% ============================================================\n131\t\\section{Experiments and Results}\n132\t\n133\tAll experiments use DeepMind Control Suite \\cite{tassa2018deepmind} environments.\n134\tThe DreamerV3 $dt=1$ baseline achieves evaluation returns of $\\sim$916 (Walker Walk), $\\sim$594 (Cheetah Run), $\\sim$785 (Cartpole Swingup), and $\\sim$213 (Hopper Hop), averaged across seeds.\n135\t\n136\t%% ---- 4.1 C1 ----\n137\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Consistently Degrades Performance (C1)}\n138\t\n139\tEvery $dt>1$ imagination configuration degrades asymptotic performance relative to the $dt=1$ baseline.\n140\tFixed $dt=2$ with trained embedding achieves peak 485 before collapsing to $\\sim$135.\n141\tFixed $dt=4$ reaches only 177 (33\\% of baseline at the same step).\n142\tA learned gating mechanism peaks at 541 before collapsing to 99.\n143\tThe degradation is monotonic in $dt$: $dt=1$ ($\\sim$950) $>$ $dt=2$ (peak 485) $>$ $dt=4$ (peak 177).\n144\tThis holds across all tested environments.\n145\t\n146\t%% ---- 4.2 C2 ----\n147\t\\subsection{Trained dt\\_embedding Reliably Triggers CIVO (C2)}\n148\t\\label{sec:c2}\n149\t\n150\tThe central diagnostic finding: trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} is the necessary and sufficient condition for CIVO.\n151\t\n152\t\\paragraph{Walker Walk cross-seed validation (008d, trained embedding).}\n153\tBoth seeds exceed NVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$ at 35K+ steps.\n154\tSeed~1 sustains elevated NVE across three consecutive checkpoints (1.71$\\times$\\,$\\to$\\,2.09$\\times$\\,$\\to$\\,4.12$\\times$) and collapses (eval 131 at 105K).\n155\tSeed~2 transiently exceeds 1.5$\\times$ at 45K (NVE\\,=\\,1.62$\\times$) but self-corrects (NVE\\,=\\,0.38$\\times$ at 105K, eval 268).\n156\t\n157\t\\begin{table}[h]\n158\t\\centering\n159\t\\caption{Walker 008d (trained dt\\_embedding, subsample ON): cross-seed NVE trajectory.}\n160\t\\label{tab:008d_cross_seed}\n161\t\\small\n162\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}rcccc@{}}\n163\t\\toprule\n164\tStep & Seed 1 NVE & Seed 1 Eval & Seed 2 NVE & Seed 2 Eval \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\t25K  & 1.78 & 167.2 & 2.05 & 141.1 \\\\\n167\t35K  & 1.71 & 146.3 & 1.24 & 164.1 \\\\\n168\t45K  & 2.09 & 140.8 & 1.62 & 141.0 \\\\\n169\t55K  & \\textbf{4.12} & 82.7  & 1.41 & 172.3 \\\\\n170\t65K  & 1.23 & 250.0 & 0.94 & 209.2 \\\\\n171\t105K & 1.57 & 131.1 & 0.38 & 267.9 \\\\\n172\t\\bottomrule\n173\t\\end{tabular}\n174\t\\end{table}\n175\t\n176\t\\paragraph{Causal control: no dt\\_embedding (008f).}\n177\tRemoving the embedding entirely ($\\text{dt\\_embed\\_dim}=0$) eliminates CIVO across 4 seeds: all NVE\\,$<$\\,1.5$\\times$ (range 0.85--1.41).\n178\tPerformance degrades (148--503, vs.\\ baseline $\\sim$916) due to reward prediction collapse (reward MAE\\,=\\,0.747, 75$\\times$ baseline), but no seed collapses via CIVO.\n179\t\n180\t\\begin{table}[h]\n181\t\\centering\n182\t\\caption{Walker 008f (dt\\_embed\\_dim=0): all seeds show NVE\\,$<$\\,1.5$\\times$, no CIVO.}\n183\t\\label{tab:008f}\n184\t\\small\n185\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n186\t\\toprule\n187\tSeed & Eval@105K & NVE max (35K+) & Collapse & CIVO \\\\\n188\t\\midrule\n189\ts0 & 503 & 0.97 & No & No \\\\\n190\ts1 & 380 & 0.85 & No & No \\\\\n191\ts2 & 148 & 1.14 & No & No \\\\\n192\ts3 & 364 & $<$1.5 & No & No \\\\\n193\ts4 & 307 & 1.41 & No & No \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table}\n197\t\n198\t\\paragraph{Frozen embedding (015b).}\n199\tFreezing the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} at random initialization ($\\text{requires\\_grad}=\\text{False}$) produces a third failure mode---gradient starvation (NVE\\,$=$\\,0.42, actor loss$\\to$0)---distinct from CIVO.\n200\tThis confirms that \\emph{training} of the embedding, not its mere presence, is the necessary condition."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYAhAGN6MCXs6YiBXL2zzL","session_id":"bc4e0f6b-485a-459f-a513-fa7886e114ae","uuid":"db6f213f-5371-4600-b888-6cd708cd2af8","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:02:19.909Z"}
