{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QNCdcYQjhFH68uaCH4LGbx","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tThis confirms that \\emph{training} of the embedding, not its mere presence, is the necessary condition.\n201\t\n202\t\\begin{table}[h]\n203\t\\centering\n204\t\\caption{dt\\_embedding state $\\times$ dt$>$1 data presence: failure mode matrix.}\n205\t\\label{tab:2x2_matrix}\n206\t\\small\n207\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n208\t\\toprule\n209\tdt\\_emb state & $dt>1$ data present & $dt>1$ data absent \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\tTrained  & \\textcolor{civo}{CIVO collapse (NVE 2--4$\\times$)} & Value suppression (NVE 0.5--0.7$\\times$) \\\\\n212\tFrozen   & Gradient starvation (NVE 0.4$\\times$) & n/a \\\\\n213\tAbsent   & No CIVO, reward degraded (NVE 0.3--1.1$\\times$) & $=$ baseline \\\\\n214\t\\bottomrule\n215\t\\end{tabular}\n216\t\\end{table}\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{RSSM fidelity gap.}\n219\tThe mechanistic basis of CIVO is a fidelity gap in the RSSM's predictions at $dt>1$.\n220\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} shows that the trained embedding (008d) amplifies the reward prediction gap to 2.78$\\times$ ($dt=2$ vs.\\ $dt=1$ MAE), creating the exploitation channel.\n221\tRemoving the embedding (008f) collapses this gap to 1.01$\\times$ (at the cost of uniformly poor reward prediction, MAE\\,=\\,0.75).\n222\t\n223\t\\begin{table}[h]\n224\t\\centering\n225\t\\caption{RSSM prediction fidelity: absolute errors and $dt=2$/$dt=1$ ratios. Both absolute values and ratios are reported; 008d's low obs gap (1.30$\\times$) is misleading because its $dt=1$ error (41.3) is already 3$\\times$ baseline---the model has collapsed.}\n226\t\\label{tab:rssm_fidelity}\n227\t\\small\n228\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n229\t\\toprule\n230\tConfig & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Obs MSE} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{Reward MAE} \\\\\n231\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n232\t & $dt$=1 & $dt$=2 & Gap & $dt$=1 & $dt$=2 & Gap \\\\\n233\t\\midrule\n234\tBaseline          & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.010 & 0.016 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n235\t008d (trained)    & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & 0.057 & 0.158 & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} \\\\\n236\t008f (no emb)     & 8.01  & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 0.747 & 0.753 & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\t%% ---- 4.3 C3 ----\n242\t\\subsection{Untrained dt\\_embedding Prevents Optimistic Drift (C3)}\n243\t\n244\tWhen the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} is present but never exposed to $dt>1$ data (subsample OFF), it cannot learn meaningful temporal discrimination.\n245\tAll 3 Walker seeds show NVE\\,$<$\\,1.0$\\times$ (range 0.51--0.66) with performance at 85\\% of baseline (eval range 631--899).\n246\tThis pattern replicates in Cheetah (3 seeds, NVE 0.48--0.50, 87\\% baseline).\n247\t\n248\t\\begin{table}[h]\n249\t\\centering\n250\t\\caption{Unexposed dt\\_embedding: cross-environment value suppression.}\n251\t\\label{tab:unexposed_cross_env}\n252\t\\small\n253\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n254\t\\toprule\n255\tEnvironment & Eval vs.\\ Baseline & NVE Range & Seeds $<$1.0$\\times$ \\\\\n256\t\\midrule\n257\tWalker Walk  & 85\\% (778/916) & 0.51--0.66 & 3/3 \\\\\n258\tCheetah Run  & 87\\% (519/594) & 0.48--0.50 & 3/3 \\\\\n259\t\\bottomrule\n260\t\\end{tabular}\n261\t\\end{table}\n262\t\n263\t%% ---- 4.4 C4+C5 ----\n264\t\\subsection{Temporal Step Size and Horizon Effects (C4, C5)}\n265\t\n266\tCIVO severity is monotonic in $dt$: $dt=2$ (peak eval 485, collapse to 135) is strictly worse than $dt=1$ ($\\sim$950) but better than $dt=4$ (peak 177, 33\\% of baseline).\n267\tThis is consistent with RSSM prediction error compounding over longer temporal gaps.\n268\t\n269\tSubsampling (shorter effective training horizons) contributes to degradation but is neither necessary nor sufficient for collapse.\n270\tWith subsample OFF and untrained embedding, $H=15$ achieves 85\\% baseline (778) while $H=8$ achieves only 26\\% (242), indicating horizon length matters independently.\n271\tCritically, with subsample ON and trained embedding, restricting imagination to $dt=1$ does not prevent collapse (Section~\\ref{sec:c6}), confirming that the root cause is embedding-mediated overestimation, not data reduction.\n272\t\n273\t%% ---- 4.5 C6 ----\n274\t\\subsection{Dual Collapse Pathways from Trained dt\\_embedding (C6)}\n275\t\\label{sec:c6}\n276\t\n277\tA key finding: restricting imagination to $dt=1$ while retaining the trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} does \\emph{not} prevent collapse.\n278\tAcross 4 independent seeds, 2/4 (50\\%) collapse---the same rate as $dt=2$ imagination.\n279\t\n280\t\\begin{table}[h]\n281\t\\centering\n282\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 (trained dt\\_emb, $dt=1$ imagination): 4-seed results. Collapse rate 50\\%.}\n283\t\\label{tab:008e_v3_seeds}\n284\t\\small\n285\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccl@{}}\n286\t\\toprule\n287\tSeed & Eval@105K & NVE Peak & Collapse & Pathway \\\\\n288\t\\midrule\n289\ts1 & 869.9 & 0.80 & No  & --- \\\\\n290\ts2 & 137.0 & 3.78 & Yes & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n291\ts3 & 92.7  & 4.02 & Yes & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n292\ts4 & 899.3 & 0.99 & No  & --- \\\\\n293\t\\bottomrule\n294\t\\end{tabular}\n295\t\\end{table}\n296\t\n297\tThe collapsed seeds exhibit dynamics \\emph{qualitatively different} from classic CIVO (Pathway~A).\n298\tWe identify two distinct pathways:\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[h]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\caption{Two collapse pathways: classic CIVO (Pathway A) vs.\\ policy instability (Pathway B).}\n303\t\\label{tab:dual_pathway}\n304\t\\small\n305\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n306\t\\toprule\n307\tFeature & Seed 1 (healthy) & 008e\\_v3 s2 (Pathway B) & 008d s0 (Pathway A) \\\\\n308\t\\midrule\n309\tEval@105K            & 869.9              & 137.0                & 82.7 \\\\\n310\tNVE@105K             & 0.8                & 3.78                 & 4.12 \\\\\n311\tValue $\\Delta$ 85K$\\to$105K & +30\\%       & +0.1\\%               & +130\\% \\\\\n312\tEval $\\Delta$ 85K$\\to$105K  & +32\\%       & $-$81\\%              & $-$44\\% \\\\\n313\tNVE driver           & n/a                & Eval denominator collapse & Value numerator explosion \\\\\n314\tEntropy trend        & Stable ($-$5.2)    & $\\uparrow$ ($-$5.2$\\to$$-$3.8)  & $\\downarrow$ ($-$3.5$\\to$$-$4.9) \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\end{table}\n318\t\n319\t\\textbf{Pathway~A} (classic CIVO): The critic overestimates $\\to$ the actor over-exploits high-valued actions $\\to$ entropy decreases $\\to$ policy becomes deterministic and wrong.\n320\t\\textbf{Pathway~B} (policy instability): The RSSM's distorted latent representations (from $dt>1$ training data, even without $dt>1$ imagination) cause inconsistent actor gradients $\\to$ entropy increases $\\to$ policy degrades toward random.\n321\t\n322\tA baseline run with the same seed (009a, seed~2) is completely stable (eval 618$\\to$596$\\to$837), ruling out seed-specific fragility.\n323\tThe trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} creates vulnerability through multiple pathways; $dt=1$ imagination eliminates Pathway~A but not Pathway~B.\n324\t\n325\tActor entropy serves as a discriminator between the three regimes:\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{table}[h]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\caption{Actor entropy trajectories distinguish three regimes.}\n330\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n331\t\\small\n332\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n333\t\\toprule\n334\tTrajectory Type & Representative & Early & Late & Direction \\\\\n335\t\\midrule\n336\tPathway A (CIVO)       & 008d s0           & $-$3.47 & $-$4.88 & $\\downarrow$ exploitation \\\\\n337\tPathway B (instability) & 008e\\_v3 s2      & $-$5.21 & $-$3.83 & $\\uparrow$ randomization \\\\\n338\tHealthy                 & baseline / 008a   & $-$4.50 & $-$5.10 & $\\to$ stable \\\\\n339\t\\bottomrule\n340\t\\end{tabular}\n341\t\\end{table}\n342\t\n343\t%% ---- 4.6 C7 ----\n344\t\\subsection{Backward-Pass Interventions Fail (C7)}\n345\t\\label{sec:c7}\n346\t\n347\tWe hypothesized that selectively stopping gradients from specific loss components through the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} (selective gradient stopping, SGS) could prevent CIVO by blocking the co-adaptation channel.\n348\tThree SGS variants were tested across 17 seeds and 2 environments:\n349\t\n350\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n351\t    \\item \\textbf{Reward-only SGS}: Detach \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} from reward loss gradients. Despite the reward head contributing the largest gradient fraction (38--49\\%), this intervention \\emph{worsens} CIVO (NVE\\,=\\,10.42$\\times$, eval collapses to 31.7). Blocking reward gradients forces co-adaptation through the observation decoder, reinforcing spatial bias.\n352\t    \\item \\textbf{Full-pathway SGS}: Block all non-KL gradients through \\texttt{dt\\_embedding}. Result: 0/3 across Walker and Cheetah. Even with only KL dynamics gradients updating the embedding, CIVO persists.\n353\t    \\item \\textbf{Obs-decoder-only SGS}: Block only observation decoder gradients (the identified causal channel for spatial bias). Result: 1/6 across Walker (4 seeds) and Cheetah (2 seeds). The single surviving Walker seed still suffers universal value collapse (value-to-eval ratio 0.056 at 215K). On Cheetah, NVE \\emph{amplifies} to 21.9--30.2$\\times$ (vs.\\ untreated 8.3$\\times$)---a compensatory amplification effect.\n354\t\\end{enumerate}\n355\t\n356\tThe aggregate result: \\textbf{1/17 seeds avoid CIVO}, and that seed still suffers critic calibration damage.\n357\tBy contrast, embedding removal (008f + 024b) achieves 100\\% CIVO prevention (5/5 seeds).\n358\t\n359\t\\textbf{Core finding}: Gradient magnitude is decoupled from causal importance.\n360\tThe reward head contributes the largest gradients (38--49\\%) but reward-only SGS fails worst.\n361\tThe observation decoder contributes the smallest non-zero gradients (16--22\\%) but targeted SGS also fails.\n362\tThe failure is fundamental: backward-pass interventions cannot undo forward-pass representational damage.\n363\tThe \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} shapes RSSM latent representations through the forward pass regardless of which loss components update it.\n364\t\n365\t%% ---- 4.7 C8 ----\n366\t\\subsection{Forward-Pass Mitigation: Temporal Conditioning Noise (C8)}\n367\t\\label{sec:c8}\n368\t\n369\tThe backward-pass failure diagnosis (Section~\\ref{sec:c7}) motivates forward-pass interventions that directly reduce the precision of temporal conditioning during imagination.\n370\tWe propose \\textbf{Temporal Conditioning Noise (TCN)}: during imagination rollouts, inject Gaussian noise $\\epsilon \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2)$ into the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} output before it conditions the RSSM.\n371\tThe noise prevents the critic from relying on precise (but inaccurate) temporal signals, disrupting the co-adaptation that underlies CIVO.\n372\t\n373\t\\paragraph{TCN $\\sigma=0.2$, Walker Walk.}\n374\tNVE remains below 1.0$\\times$ throughout training (peak 0.908 at 95K, terminal 0.095 at 215K).\n375\tEvaluation peaks at 821.1 (90\\% of baseline), demonstrating that CIVO prevention does not require sacrificing performance.\n376\tThis is the first intervention across the entire experimental campaign to achieve complete CIVO prevention with competitive performance.\n377\t\n378\t\\paragraph{TCN $\\sigma=0.5$, Walker Walk (2 seeds).}\n379\tExcessive noise creates an oscillatory bistable regime: NVE peaks at 1.6--4.0$\\times$, evaluation plateaus at $\\sim$365 (40\\% baseline).\n380\tUseful temporal information is destroyed along with the pathological signal.\n381\t\n382\t\\paragraph{TCN $\\sigma=0.2$, Cheetah Run.}\n383\tInsufficient dose: NVE\\,=\\,1.903 at 55K (just exceeds 1.5$\\times$ threshold).\n384\tCheetah's untreated CIVO severity is 10--35$\\times$ that of Walker (NVE 8--70$\\times$ vs.\\ 2--4$\\times$), requiring a proportionally larger noise dose.\n385\t\n386\t\\paragraph{TCN $\\sigma=0.5$, Cheetah Run.}\n387\tNVE reduced from untreated 8--108$\\times$ to 1.5--4$\\times$---a substantial reduction though not complete prevention.\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Dose-response relationship.}\n390\tThe required noise magnitude $\\sigma$ correlates positively with untreated CIVO severity:\n391\tWalker (untreated NVE 2--4$\\times$) is treated by $\\sigma=0.2$;\n392\tCheetah (untreated NVE 8--70$\\times$) requires $\\sigma=0.5$.\n393\tThis provides mechanistic insight: the noise must be sufficient to degrade temporal conditioning precision below the threshold at which the critic can exploit RSSM fidelity gaps.\n394\t\n395\tWe also tested \\textbf{RAVE (Reality-Anchored Value Estimation)}, which caps imagined values using an EMA of real environment values.\n396\tRAVE fails because the EMA anchor itself becomes polluted during CIVO onset---early overestimated values shift the anchor upward, rendering the ceiling ineffective.\n397\t\n398\tAn \\textbf{uncertainty-gated imagination truncation} pilot (truncating imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds a threshold) achieves partial reduction (NVE from 2--4$\\times$ to $\\sim$1.8$\\times$, eval 380 at 105K) but is less effective than TCN.\n399\tThis independently confirms that forward-pass interventions are the correct layer.\n400\t\n401\t%% ---- 4.8 Cross-env ----\n402\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment Generalization}\n403\t\\label{sec:cross_env}\n404\t\n405\tCIVO generalizes across all 4 tested environments, including the non-locomotion task Cartpole Swingup.\n406\t\n407\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run: amplified severity.}\n408\tCheetah's more complex dynamics (6-DOF vs.\\ Walker's 2-DOF) and steeper reward landscape amplify CIVO: NVE reaches 8--70$\\times$ (vs.\\ Walker 2--4$\\times$) with 100\\% collapse rate (3/3 seeds).\n409\t\n410\t\\begin{table}[h]\n411\t\\centering\n412\t\\caption{CIVO severity scales with environment complexity.}\n413\t\\label{tab:severity_cross_env}\n414\t\\small\n415\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n416\t\\toprule\n417\tEnvironment & NVE Range & Collapse Rate & Typical Onset \\\\\n418\t\\midrule\n419\tWalker Walk      & 2--4$\\times$   & $\\sim$50\\% (3/6)  & 35--55K \\\\\n420\tCartpole Swingup & 1.9--4.5$\\times$ & $\\sim$67\\% (2/3) & 45--55K \\\\\n421\tHopper Hop       & 2.8--7.1$\\times$ & $\\sim$67\\% (2/3) & 45--60K \\\\\n422\tCheetah Run      & 8--70$\\times$  & 100\\% (3/3)        & 45--65K \\\\\n423\t\\bottomrule\n424\t\\end{tabular}\n425\t\\end{table}\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup: CIVO in non-locomotion tasks.}\n428\tAll 3 seeds with trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} exceed NVE\\,1.5$\\times$ (range 1.85--4.5$\\times$), with 2/3 collapsing.\n429\tThis confirms the mechanism is architecture-level (RSSM + \\texttt{dt\\_embedding}), not task-specific.\n430\t\n431\t\\begin{table}[h]\n432\t\\centering\n433\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup: trained dt\\_embedding triggers CIVO in non-locomotion tasks.}\n434\t\\label{tab:cartpole_civo}\n435\t\\small\n436\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n437\t\\toprule\n438\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval@105K & NVE Peak & Collapse \\\\\n439\t\\midrule\n440\ts0 & 520 & 95  & 3.20 & Yes \\\\\n441\ts1 & 610 & 580 & 1.85 & No (self-corrected) \\\\\n442\ts2 & 480 & 70  & 4.50 & Yes \\\\\n443\t\\bottomrule\n444\t\\end{tabular}\n445\t\\end{table}\n446\t\n447\t\\paragraph{NVE 1.5$\\times$ diagnostic threshold.}\n448\tAcross all 4 environments, the NVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$ threshold at 35K+ steps achieves 26/26 correct classifications (Table~\\ref{tab:nve_threshold_4env}).\n449\t\n450\t\\begin{table}[h]\n451\t\\centering\n452\t\\caption{NVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$ threshold: cross-environment classification (trained dt\\_emb exceeds threshold vs.\\ untrained/absent stays below). AUROC\\,=\\,0.969.}\n453\t\\label{tab:nve_threshold_4env}\n454\t\\small\n455\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n456\t\\toprule\n457\tEnvironment & Trained $>$1.5$\\times$ & Untrained $<$1.5$\\times$ & Total Correct \\\\\n458\t\\midrule\n459\tWalker Walk      & 5/5 & 4/4 & 9/9 \\\\\n460\tCheetah Run      & 3/3 & 2/2 & 5/5 \\\\\n461\tCartpole Swingup & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n462\tHopper Hop       & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n463\t\\midrule\n464\t\\textbf{Total}   & \\textbf{14/14} & \\textbf{12/12} & \\textbf{26/26} \\\\\n465\t\\bottomrule\n466\t\\end{tabular}\n467\t\\end{table}\n468\t\n469\tThreshold sensitivity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity}) confirms 1.5$\\times$ as the optimal operating point (F1\\,=\\,0.95), balancing false positives (transient spikes at lower thresholds) against false negatives (missed borderline cases at higher thresholds).\n470\t\n471\t\\begin{table}[h]\n472\t\\centering\n473\t\\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity analysis.}\n474\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}\n475\t\\small\n476\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n477\t\\toprule\n478\tThreshold & Accuracy & FP & FN & F1 \\\\\n479\t\\midrule\n480\t1.3$\\times$ & 0.88 & 2 & 0 & 0.90 \\\\\n481\t\\textbf{1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{0.94} & \\textbf{0} & \\textbf{1} & \\textbf{0.95} \\\\\n482\t1.7$\\times$ & 0.91 & 0 & 2 & 0.91 \\\\\n483\t2.0$\\times$ & 0.84 & 0 & 4 & 0.83 \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{Cross-environment causal control.}\n489\tRemoving \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} eliminates CIVO across all environments (0/9+ seeds exceed NVE 1.5$\\times$), with consistent reward degradation:\n490\t\n491\t\\begin{table}[h]\n492\t\\centering\n493\t\\caption{No-dt-embedding causal control: CIVO eliminated across all environments.}\n494\t\\label{tab:no_emb_cross_env}\n495\t\\small\n496\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n497\t\\toprule\n498\tEnvironment & Seeds Tested & NVE $>$1.5$\\times$ & CIVO Rate \\\\\n499\t\\midrule\n500\tWalker Walk      & 4+ & 0 & 0\\% \\\\\n501\tCheetah Run      & 2+ & 0 & 0\\% \\\\\n502\tCartpole Swingup & 3  & 0 & 0\\% \\\\\n503\t\\midrule\n504\tTotal            & 9+ & 0 & 0\\% \\\\\n505\t\\bottomrule\n506\t\\end{tabular}\n507\t\\end{table}\n508\t\n509\t%% ============================================================\n510\t%%  5. DISCUSSION\n511\t%% ============================================================\n512\t\\section{Discussion}\n513\t\n514\t\\paragraph{The CIVO mechanism.}\n515\tCIVO arises from the interaction of two factors: (1) the RSSM's inherently lower prediction fidelity at $dt>1$ (obs MSE gap 2.04$\\times$ even in the baseline), and (2) a trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} that enables the critic to exploit this gap through precise temporal conditioning.\n516\tThe result is a positive feedback loop: the critic overestimates values at $dt>1$ $\\to$ the actor shifts its policy toward these overestimated regions $\\to$ distribution shift degrades RSSM predictions $\\to$ further overestimation.\n517\t\n518\t\\paragraph{Why MPC succeeds where imagination fails.}\n519\tTAWM \\cite{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates successful temporal conditioning in TD-MPC2's MPC framework.\n520\tThe key difference: MPC re-plans from real environment states at each step, breaking the positive feedback loop.\n521\tIn imagination-based actor-critic, the critic bootstraps from its own imagined predictions, enabling CIVO's self-reinforcing dynamics.\n522\tThis architectural distinction---not temporal conditioning per se---determines whether variable-rate planning succeeds.\n523\t\n524\t\\paragraph{Forward-pass vs.\\ backward-pass interventions.}\n525\tThe comprehensive failure of gradient-level interventions (1/17 seeds across 3 SGS variants, Section~\\ref{sec:c7}) establishes that CIVO is fundamentally a forward-pass phenomenon.\n526\tThe \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} shapes RSSM latent representations through the forward pass; its influence persists regardless of gradient routing.\n527\tCompensatory amplification (blocking one gradient channel strengthens co-adaptation through others) further confirms this conclusion.\n528\tTCN succeeds precisely because it operates at the forward-pass level, degrading the conditioning precision that enables exploitation without modifying gradient flow.\n529\t\n530\t\\paragraph{Dose-response and calibration.}\n531\tThe TCN dose-response---Walker ($\\sigma=0.2$) vs.\\ Cheetah ($\\sigma=0.5$)---suggests that the required noise scales with the severity of the untreated CIVO, which in turn scales with RSSM fidelity gap magnitude.\n532\tThis is consistent with the mechanistic model: noise must degrade temporal conditioning precision below the exploitation threshold, and larger fidelity gaps require more noise to achieve this.\n533\tAn adaptive noise schedule that calibrates $\\sigma$ to measured RSSM fidelity could eliminate the need for per-environment tuning.\n534\t\n535\t\\paragraph{Multiple vulnerability pathways.}\n536\tThe 008e\\_v3 results (Section~\\ref{sec:c6}) reveal that trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} creates vulnerability through at least two pathways.\n537\tPathway~A (classic CIVO) operates through value overestimation and is eliminated by $dt=1$ imagination.\n538\tPathway~B (policy instability) operates through RSSM latent distortion from $dt>1$ training data and persists even with $dt=1$ imagination.\n539\tThis dual-pathway structure implies that surface-level fixes (restricting imagination dt) are insufficient; the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} itself must be either removed or regularized (as TCN does).\n540\t\n541\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n542\tAll experiments use DreamerV3 with RSSM.\n543\tWhile the CIVO mechanism likely applies to other latent-dynamics models with temporal conditioning, we do not test this.\n544\tThe TCN dose requires environment-specific tuning ($\\sigma=0.2$ for Walker, $\\sigma=0.5$ for Cheetah); adaptive calibration is left to future work.\n545\tThe NVE diagnostic threshold (1.5$\\times$) is validated on DMC tasks; applicability to higher-dimensional observation spaces or sparse-reward tasks is untested.\n546\t\n547\t%% ============================================================\n548\t%%  6. CONCLUSION\n549\t%% ============================================================\n550\t\\section{Conclusion}\n551\t\n552\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of why temporal abstraction fails in DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic.\n553\tThe root cause is \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO): a trained \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} enables the critic to exploit RSSM fidelity gaps at $dt>1$, triggering a positive feedback loop that collapses the policy.\n554\tOur NVE\\,$\\geq$\\,1.5$\\times$ diagnostic achieves 26/26 classification across 4 environments.\n555\tThe exhaustive failure of backward-pass (gradient) interventions across 17 seeds establishes that CIVO is forward-pass representational damage, not gradient contamination.\n556\tTemporal Conditioning Noise (TCN), a forward-pass intervention that injects calibrated noise into the \\texttt{dt\\_embedding} during imagination, successfully prevents CIVO in Walker ($\\sigma=0.2$, 90\\% baseline performance) and substantially reduces it in Cheetah ($\\sigma=0.5$).\n557\t\n558\tThe actionable takeaway: variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3-class architectures requires either (a)~removing temporal conditioning from the world model, at significant performance cost, or (b)~regularizing temporal conditioning precision via forward-pass noise injection.\n559\tHierarchical approaches that maintain separate dynamics per temporal scale (e.g., THICK \\cite{kim2024thick}, Hieros \\cite{robine2024hieros}) may avoid flat-conditioning failure entirely and represent a promising direction.\n560\t\n561\t%% ============================================================\n562\t%%  REFERENCES\n563\t%% ============================================================\n564\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n565\t\n566\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n567\t\n568\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2023)]{hafner2023mastering}\n569\tD.~Hafner, J.~Pasukonis, J.~Ba, and T.~Lillicrap.\n570\t\\newblock Mastering diverse domains through world models.\n571\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.04104}, 2023.\n572\t\n573\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2021)]{hafner2021mastering}\n574\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, M.~Norouzi, and J.~Ba.\n575\t\\newblock Mastering {A}tari with discrete world models.\n576\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n577\t\n578\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2020)]{hafner2020dream}\n579\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, J.~Ba, and M.~Norouzi.\n580\t\\newblock Dream to control: Learning behaviors by latent imagination.\n581\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n582\t\n583\t\\bibitem[Sutton et~al.(1999)]{sutton1999between}\n584\tR.~S. Sutton, D.~Precup, and S.~Singh.\n585\t\\newblock Between {MDP}s and semi-{MDP}s: A framework for temporal abstraction in reinforcement learning.\n586\t\\newblock \\emph{Artificial Intelligence}, 112(1--2):181--211, 1999.\n587\t\n588\t\\bibitem[Precup(2000)]{precup2000temporal}\n589\tD.~Precup.\n590\t\\newblock \\emph{Temporal Abstraction in Reinforcement Learning}.\n591\t\\newblock PhD thesis, University of Massachusetts Amherst, 2000.\n592\t\n593\t\\bibitem[Sharma et~al.(2017)]{sharma2017learning}\n594\tS.~Sharma, A.~S. Lakshminarayanan, and B.~Ravindran.\n595\t\\newblock Learning to repeat: Fine grained action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n596\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2017.\n597\t\n598\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et~al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}\n599\tA.~S. Lakshminarayanan, S.~Sharma, and B.~Ravindran.\n600\t\\newblock Dynamic action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n601\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2017.\n602\t\n603\t\\bibitem[Micheli et~al.(2023)]{micheli2023transformers}\n604\tV.~Micheli, E.~Alonso, and F.~Fleuret.\n605\t\\newblock Transformers are sample-efficient world models.\n606\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n607\t\n608\t\\bibitem[Robine et~al.(2023)]{robine2023transformer}\n609\tJ.~Robine, M.~H{\\\"o}ftmann, T.~Uelwer, and S.~Harmeling.\n610\t\\newblock Transformer-based world models are happy with 100k interactions.\n611\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2023.\n612\t\n613\t\\bibitem[Nhu et~al.(2025)]{nhu2025tawm}\n614\tQ.~Nhu, T.~Lan, and B.~Boots.\n615\t\\newblock {TAWM}: Temporally-aware world models for model-based reinforcement learning.\n616\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2025.\n617\t\n618\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2018)]{lu2018non}\n619\tT.~Lu, D.~Schuurmans, and C.~Boutilier.\n620\t\\newblock Non-delusional {Q}-learning and value-iteration.\n621\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2018.\n622\t\n623\t\\bibitem[Janner et~al.(2019)]{janner2019trust}\n624\tM.~Janner, J.~Fu, M.~Zhang, and S.~Levine.\n625\t\\newblock When to trust your model: Model-based policy optimization.\n626\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2019.\n627\t\n628\t\\bibitem[Tassa et~al.(2018)]{tassa2018deepmind}\n629\tY.~Tassa, Y.~Doron, A.~Muldal, T.~Erez, Y.~Li, D.~de~Las~Casas, D.~Budden, A.~Abdolmaleki, J.~Merel, A.~Lefrancq, T.~Lillicrap, and M.~Riedmiller.\n630\t\\newblock {D}eep{M}ind {C}ontrol {S}uite.\n631\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:1801.00690}, 2018.\n632\t\n633\t\\bibitem[Agarwal et~al.(2021)]{agarwal2021deep}\n634\tR.~Agarwal, M.~Schwarzer, P.~S. Castro, A.~C. Courville, and M.~Bellemare.\n635\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning at the edge of the statistical precipice.\n636\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2021.\n637\t\n638\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2024)]{kim2024thick}\n639\tS.~Kim, S.~Lee, and G.~Lee.\n640\t\\newblock {THICK}: Temporal hierarchical imagination for model-based control.\n641\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n642\t\n643\t\\bibitem[Robine et~al.(2024)]{robine2024hieros}\n644\tJ.~Robine, M.~H{\\\"o}ftmann, T.~Uelwer, and S.~Harmeling.\n645\t\\newblock {H}ieros: Hierarchical imagination on structured state space sequence world models.\n646\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n647\t\n648\t\\bibitem[Henderson et~al.(2018)]{henderson2018deep}\n649\tP.~Henderson, R.~Islam, P.~Bachman, J.~Pineau, D.~Precup, and D.~Meger.\n650\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning that matters.\n651\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2018.\n652\t\n653\t\\end{thebibliography}\n654\t\n655\t\\end{document}\n656\t"}]},"parent_tool_